// ============================================================================
// HUMAN-AUTHORED CONCEPTS — NOT WRITTEN OR EDITED BY AN LLM
// ============================================================================
//
// Ctrl-F ANCHORS (this file is deliberately structured for keyboard navigation):
//
//   "BEGIN RELIGION"   line 47   ->  "END RELIGION"   line 53
//   "BEGIN CONTESTED"  line 55   ->  "END CONTESTED"  line 61
//   "BEGIN OTHER"      line 63   ->  "END OTHER"      line 66
//
// Everything between a BEGIN and its END is yours. Nothing in this file is
// generated, and no agent is permitted to write into it — that is the point.
// `scripts/stage_review.py` and the build treat it as a normal concept source,
// so entries here merge with the rest of the map with no special handling.
//
// FORMAT (identical to seed_concepts.jsonl):
//   {"label": "...", "definition": "...", "domain": "..."}
//   - one sentence, ~12-22 words, describes what it IS and DOES
//   - no hedging ("refers to", "the idea that"), British spelling
//   - `domain` may be a new string; it does not have to be one of the 18
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// EDITORIAL STANDARD FOR THIS FILE (as specified by the repository owner)
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// 1. POLITICAL STANCE: cold realism. Describe how a thing actually operates and
//    what it actually costs, without approving or condemning it. Treat power,
//    interest and constraint as ordinary facts.
//
// 2. ECONOMIC BASELINE: regulated market economy. Neither anarcho-capitalism
//    nor central planning is treated as the default against which others are
//    deviations. Both are describable; neither is the frame.
//
// 3. SOCIAL BASELINE: the mainstream standard of roughly 1960, used as a fixed
//    reference point rather than as an endorsement. The purpose is a stable
//    zero, not a moral claim.
//
// 4. OMIT BY DEFAULT: topics that are politically contested in current practice
//    AND carry no cross-field structural value — sexual ethics being the clear
//    case. A concept earns its place here only if its STRUCTURE transfers to
//    another field. If the only reason to include it is that it is argued
//    about, leave it out.
//
// 5. WHEN IN DOUBT, SAY LESS. An omitted concept costs coverage. A tendentious
//    one costs the credibility of every other row in the map.
//
// === BEGIN RELIGION ===
// Owner-authored. Rough structural framing is fine; these are mechanisms
// (covenant, sacrifice, orthodoxy/heresy boundary maintenance, canon formation,
// schism, apostolic succession, sabbath, tithe) rather than truth claims about
// any faith. Describe the institution and its dynamics, not its validity.

// === END RELIGION ===

// === BEGIN CONTESTED ===
// Owner-authored. Politically live concepts that DO carry transferable
// structure and therefore earn inclusion despite the contest around them.
// Apply the editorial standard above. If a row cannot be written flatly, it
// does not belong in the map.

// === END CONTESTED ===

